@prefix : <http://example.org/orexis/road#> .
@prefix courier: <http://example.org/orexis/courier#> .
@prefix sim: <http://example.org/orexis/sim#> .
@prefix orexis: <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .

<> a orexis:OntologyGraph ;    # this document is one graph, and this is what it is
    owl:imports <../courier/ontology.ttl> ,    # the words a road is laid out in: a position, two coordinates on a thing, and what no van does
                <../sim/ontology.ttl> .        # the hedges stand where their model says, played by the simulator

#  THE ROAD, A DOMAIN.
#  One stretch of road, shared by every world posed on it: a single-track lane with a passing place, open
#  road two cells wide at each end. The grid is infinite and nothing authors it
#  (the-grid-is-infinite-and-a-position-is-two-coordinates): the road is not cells but what STANDS on it,
#  the hedges either side of the lane, each a thing at two coordinates with no instrument of its own, which
#  a van's radar sees within its range and its courier then believes an obstacle at - and plans round,
#  since no van stands where an obstacle is. No agent is told the hedges: a courier plans across the plane
#  as if it were open, sees a hedge when it comes within range, and its plan is reopened where that makes
#  the next step's world impossible (#921). The road is discovered, not known. What it is NOT is a world:
#  it says nothing of who drives on it, where a van stands or what is owed where. `world/lane/` poses two
#  couriers on it, `world/layby/` one courier and a van parked by a driver nobody plays, the same road under
#  different initial states; a third world would import this document and author its vans.
#
#  THE SHAPE: columns 0 and 1 and columns 5 and 6 are open, three rows deep; columns 2 to 4 are the lane,
#  row 1, with the passing place at (3, 2); so the hedges stand at (2, 0), (3, 0), (4, 0), (2, 2) and
#  (4, 2). A van told any neighbour drives there, hedge or not: the simulator refuses nothing but a cell
#  two away, and what keeps a plan off a hedge is the constraint, weighed where the radar has shown one.

:hedge_2_0 sim:simulatedBy [ a sim:Model ; sim:x 2 ; sim:y 0 ] ; rdfs:label "the hedge north of the lane's first cell" .
:hedge_3_0 sim:simulatedBy [ a sim:Model ; sim:x 3 ; sim:y 0 ] ; rdfs:label "the hedge north of the lane's second cell" .
:hedge_4_0 sim:simulatedBy [ a sim:Model ; sim:x 4 ; sim:y 0 ] ; rdfs:label "the hedge north of the lane's third cell" .
:hedge_2_2 sim:simulatedBy [ a sim:Model ; sim:x 2 ; sim:y 2 ] ; rdfs:label "the hedge south of the lane's first cell" .
:hedge_4_2 sim:simulatedBy [ a sim:Model ; sim:x 4 ; sim:y 2 ] ; rdfs:label "the hedge south of the lane's third cell, east of the passing place" .
